Next coverage audit: kernel and base
===================================

Status: initial coverage implemented in all 21 areas by the third expansion.
See COVERAGE-THIRD for the exact implemented contracts, omissions, and runtime
qualification limits; the detailed bullets below remain the broader audit.
The objective is enough semantic coverage to write new scripts without
first modifying the subsystem. This is not a claim of complete coverage.

FBT, pid, syscall and other dynamic providers can already trace many routines
without new static probes. SDT/USDT adds explicit reasons, usable operation
identities and stable semantic boundaries that function tracing may miss.
A zero count of literal SDT calls is screening evidence, not proof an area
is untraceable: inspect wrappers, headers, provider definitions and callers.
Only the configured build and live enumeration establish available probes.

Coverage contract for each added operation
-----------------------------------------
* Start and completion with the same identity; completion on success,
  failure, timeout, interruption and cancellation wherever the operation
  actually begins. Document validation that precedes start.
* Distinguish queue residence, worker execution and asynchronous completion.
  A function returning after submission is not completion of the I/O.
* Include operation/result, requested/actual quantity, and failure/retry
  reason. Preserve errno, protocol and library status domains separately.
* Correlate nested calls and repeated/reused objects: thread + nesting or
  explicit sequence/generation, as appropriate. Record identity before free.
* State-change and drop events are single events; they do not need artificial
  start/end pairs. Do not count retries or layers as unique requests.
* No unconditional timestamp, string formatting, allocation, lock or payload
  copy merely to feed a probe. Both enabled and disabled builds must work.
* Ship argument documentation, source inventory, companion example profile,
  and tests. Compile/link both modes, enumerate live probes, and exercise
  representative success/failure/cancel paths before calling coverage live.

Priority A: next implementation set
-----------------------------------
1. tmpfs vnode/reclaim and capacity
   sys/fs/tmpfs/tmpfs_subr.c: tmpfs_alloc_vp, tmpfs_alloc_node,
   tmpfs_reg_resize; tmpfs_vnops.c: tmpfs_rename_relock.
   No direct SDT/USDT sites found in this subtree. Pair vnode allocation,
   reclaim waits and rename relocking; expose restart reasons, space/node
   limit refusals, requested size and result. tmpfs_alloc_vp explicitly
   sleeps on TMPFS_VNODE_WRECLAIM and retries vget/raced vnode identity.
   Script: tmpfs-stalls.d. Existing generic vnode probes are insufficient to
   distinguish these branches. Consult the recorded WITNESS issue before
   instrumenting related detach/reclaim ordering; this audit diagnoses none.

2. FAT/msdosfs allocation and integrity recovery
   sys/fs/msdosfs/msdosfs_fat.c: clusteralloc1, fatentry;
   msdosfs_vfsops.c: msdosfs_integrity_error, msdosfs_remount_ro.
   No direct sites found. Pair allocation/chain extension and sync, report
   free-cluster search/failure, corruption detection and read-only transition
   reason/result. Script: fat-pressure.d. Use mount/cluster identities and
   counts, not file contents.

3. NFS client state recovery
   sys/fs/nfsclient/nfs_clstate.c: nfscl_recover, nfscl_renewthread,
   nfscl_open, nfscl_lockrelease.
   No direct sites found in the client subtree. Pair recovery/renewal,
   expose reclaim stages, delegation recall and open/lock state outcomes.
   Include mount/client identity, generation, operation and NFS status.
   Script: nfs-recovery.d. The newly added RPC probes cover transport retry,
   not NFS lease/reclaim semantics.

4. NFS server compound and session state
   sys/fs/nfsserver/nfs_nfsdsocket.c: nfsrvd_compound;
   nfs_nfsdstate.c: nfsrv_getclient.
   No direct sites found in the server subtree. Pair compound request and
   individual operations, report replay/session/sequence decisions and
   final protocol status; use request identity plus operation index.
   Script: nfs-server-latency.d. Keep duplicate requests distinguishable.

5. Swap and page reclamation phases
   sys/vm/swap_pager.c: swap_pager_getpages_locked, swap_pager_putpages,
   I/O completion callbacks; vm_pageout.c: vm_pageout_scan_inactive.
   Existing swap on/off and vm lowmem-scan events do not explain page-I/O
   completion or reclamation effectiveness. Pair actual I/O and scan/laundry
   phases; report domain, shortage, scanned/freed/dirty/stuck pages and result.
   Script: reclaim-efficiency.d. Existing swap-activity uses function probes.

6. IOMMU mapping and invalidation
   sys/dev/iommu/iommu_gas.c: iommu_gas_map;
   busdma_iommu.c: iommu_bus_dmamap_complete, iommu_domain_unload_task.
   No direct sites found. Pair address-space reservation, mapping, queued
   unload and completion; expose reservation vs page-table ENOMEM, deferred
   work and invalidation completion. Script: iommu-latency.d. Complements
   bounce-buffer probes: these are separate DMA implementations.

7. ACPI suspend/resume phases
   sys/dev/acpica/acpi.c: acpi_suspend, acpi_resume,
   acpi_EnterSleepStatePrep, acpi_EnterSleepState.
   No direct sites found in the native wrapper subtree. Pair suspend/resume
   phases, device callbacks and firmware transitions; report sleep type,
   phase, device and ACPI status/errno separately. Script: suspend-phases.d.
   Start at native boundaries around imported ACPICA. Check DTrace clock
   behavior across sleep before claiming whole-suspend duration.

8. Kernel crypto dispatch
   sys/opencrypto/crypto.c: crypto_newsession, crypto_dispatch_one,
   crypto_invoke, crypto_done.
   The framework core lacks direct sites, although other opencrypto files
   have probes. Pair submit/driver invocation/true completion by cryptop,
   report hardware/software driver, queue/restart reason and result.
   Script: crypto-queue.d. KTLS probes cover only one framework consumer.
   Do not expose keys, IV contents or cleartext.

9. libc resolver and NSS
   lib/libc/resolv/res_send.c: res_nsend, send_dg, send_vc;
   lib/libc/net/nsdispatch.c: _nsdispatch.
   No direct sites found in the reviewed resolver/NSS sources. Pair overall
   lookup and individual backend/server attempts; report backend selection,
   timeout, retransmit, TCP fallback, DNS result and NSS status separately.
   _nsdispatch has nested fallback dispatch, so use depth/attempt identities.
   Scripts: resolver-attempts.d, nss-latency.d. Avoid raw queries/password data.

10. libthr synchronization
    lib/libthr/thread/thr_mutex.c: mutex_lock_common; thr_cond.c; thr_join.c.
    No direct sites found in libthr. Pair contended mutex/condition/join
    waits, report spin-to-sleep transitions, timeout/cancel/robust outcomes.
    Script: pthread-contention.d. Kernel umtx observes sleeping/waking but
    cannot explain userspace adaptive spinning and fast-path decisions.

11. Runtime linker
    libexec/rtld-elf/rtld.c: load_object, dlopen_object, dlclose_locked;
    architecture relocation and symbol-resolution entry points.
    No direct sites found in the reviewed tree. Pair load, dependency search,
    relocation and initializer execution; expose lookup/cache/fallback and
    failure reasons with object identity. Script: rtld-startup.d.
    Use bootstrap-safe USDT integration with no new libc/allocator dependency;
    explicitly test initial process loading as well as dlopen/dlclose.

12. OpenPAM dispatcher
    contrib/openpam/lib/libpam/openpam_dispatch.c: openpam_dispatch.
    Individual native PAM modules already have USDT wrappers. The common
    dispatcher lacks equivalent semantic events for chain selection, skipped
    functions, PAM_IGNORE, sufficient/requisite short-circuit and final result.
    Pair the chain and each module invocation, including dispatch errors.
    Script: pam-chain.d. Record primitive, module index/control flag and PAM
    result; never conversation answers or authentication tokens.

